{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Epic-level consolidation: TWO of this epic's caps are gated on the same host reboot, and that is an operator decision rather than an implementation gap.\n\nS4 (`/var/tmp`) and S5 (container writable layers) hit the same constraint independently, from opposite directions, and both concluded the same thing: the enforcement mechanism they wanted needs an XFS project quota on the filesystem backing the resource, that filesystem is ROOT in both cases, and XFS reads quota options at MOUNT time and refuses them on a remount. So each needs `rootflags=pquota` and a reboot before its hard ceiling can exist at all. S5's report also records the daemon refusing it in one line — `--storage-opt is supported only for overlay over xfs with 'pquota' mount option` — which is the mechanism confirming the constraint rather than an inference about it.\n\nWhat that means for the epic's claim. All seven stories now have changes pushed, so the epic is complete as AUTHORED. But the ceiling it promises is not uniformly a bound in production today:\n\n- Bounded and in service: the BuildKit budget (S0/S6). Measured pinned at 99-100% of its 5 GiB share, which is the cap working — the number is bounded because something bounds it.\n- Bounded for the debris it can reach, NOT for the live set: S5's reaper. Its own report is explicit that it \"cannot reclaim a RUNNING container's writable layer at all\", so for the live compose set it ships measurement and an alarm rather than a bound, with a stated escape rate of ~7.2 MB/s sustained net fill against a 125 MB/s volume baseline.\n- Not in service pending the reboot: S4's `/var/tmp` hard quota, and S5's per-container quota.\n\nTwo honesty notes, because the difference matters when someone later asks whether this epic delivered.\n\nFirst, both stories published an unalarmed truth metric rather than asserting the cap was live — `container_quota_enforceable` publishes 0 until the reboot, and S4's `var_tmp_hard_quota_in_effect` does the equivalent. That is the right shape: the gap is VISIBLE rather than silent, which is exactly the failure mode this epic exists to close. `var_tmp_cleanup_active` reading 0 across all 33 samples tonight is that design working as intended, not a defect.\n\nSecond, the reboot is genuinely the operator's call and I am not scheduling it. Rebooting the production Gerrit host takes Gerrit, the MCP server, and the review-bot down together, and the epic's remaining benefit does not justify an unattended overnight reboot. Recording it here so the decision is visible with its cost rather than discovered later as an unexplained \"the cap does not work\".\n\nLive context for that decision, measured this tick: root filesystem is at 70% and climbing (48.6% -> 70% over roughly 12 hours). The docker restart at 05:23 UTC produced a ~1-point dip and did not change the slope. A diagnosis of what is actually producing the growth is in flight; until it reports, the reboot's urgency is unknown, and I would rather the operator decide against a named producer than against a trend line."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788598295349375726,"uuid":"0524c36f-e4fa-487f-8ed7-dc81d6411d92"}